(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_chemistry_chemicalkinetics=[{id:"neet-2026r-q056",pn:56,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Arrhenius equation and plot",q:`For an elementary chemical reaction, the Arrhenius plot is given below.
If the energy of activation is 6.64 kJ mol\u207B\xB9 and R = 8.3 J K\u207B\xB9 mol\u207B\xB9, the temperature at which the rate constant becomes e\xB2 min\u207B\xB9, is`,o:["150 K","200 K","250 K","125 K"],c:1,s:`ln k = ln A \u2013 Ea/RT.
The graph shows ln k = 6 at the intercept (1/T = 0), so ln A = 6.
For k = e\xB2, ln k = 2.
2 = 6 \u2013 (6.64\xD710\xB3)/(8.3 \xD7 T).
(6.64\xD710\xB3)/(8.3 \xD7 T) = 4.
T = (6.64\xD710\xB3)/(8.3 \xD7 4) = 200 K.`,fig:{w:300,h:204,alt:"Arrhenius plot: ln k on the vertical axis (ticks 2, 4, 6, 8) against 1/T; a straight line falls from ln k = 6 on the vertical axis to about ln k = 1 at the right.",d:[["l",60,170,60,8],["l",60,170,280,170],["l",56,152,60,152],["l",56,134,60,134],["t",52,138,"2","e"],["l",56,116,60,116],["l",56,98,60,98],["t",52,102,"4","e"],["l",56,80,60,80],["l",56,62,60,62],["t",52,66,"6","e"],["l",56,44,60,44],["l",56,26,60,26],["t",52,30,"8","e"],["l",60,62,250,150.2,"B"],["l",22,125,22,71,"a"],["t",30,54.8,"lnk","s"],["l",120,192,148,192,"a"],["t",154,196,"1/T"]]}},{id:"neet-2026-q056",pn:56,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Units of rate constant",q:`Match List I with List II.
List-I (Order of reaction):
A. Zero order
B. First order
C. Second order
D. Third order
List-II (Unit of rate constant):
I. mol\u207B\xB9 L s\u207B\xB9
II. mol\u207B\xB2 L\xB2 s\u207B\xB9
III. s\u207B\xB9
IV. mol L\u207B\xB9 s\u207B\xB9
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],c:2,s:`The unit of the rate constant of an nth order reaction is (mol/L)^(1\u2013n) s\u207B\xB9.
Zero order, n = 0: unit is mol L\u207B\xB9 s\u207B\xB9.
First order, n = 1: unit is s\u207B\xB9.
Second order, n = 2: unit is mol\u207B\xB9 L s\u207B\xB9.
Third order, n = 3: unit is mol\u207B\xB2 L\xB2 s\u207B\xB9.
(The publisher wrote n = 2 for the third-order line, but the unit given is the n = 3 one.)
Note: Printed option 3 reads D-I, but third order matches II (mol\u207B\xB2 L\xB2 s\u207B\xB9); printed answer [3] kept (A-IV, B-III, C-I are right, D looks like a typo)`},{id:"neet-2026r-q067",pn:67,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Zero order reaction",q:"2A \u2192 B (rate constant k) is a zero-order reaction, where k = 1.0 mol L\u207B\xB9 min\u207B\xB9. If the initial concentration of A is 2 M, then the time taken to complete 75% of the reaction will be",o:["0.75 min","1.0 min","2.0 min","1.5 min"],c:0,s:`For the zero order reaction, \u2212(1/2)(dA/dt) = k.
t = (A\u2080 \u2212 A\u209C)/2k.
75% complete leaves A\u209C = 0.5 M.
t = (2 \u2212 0.5)/2 = 0.75 min.`},{id:"neet-2026-q083",pn:83,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Zero order reaction graph",q:"For a certain reaction R \u2192 Product, the plot of concentration [R] vs time has a negative slope as shown. The order of reaction is :",o:["0","1","2","2.5"],c:0,s:`For a zero order reaction, [R] = [R\u2080] \u2013 kt.
So a straight line with a negative slope (k = \u2013slope) shows a zero order reaction.`,fig:{w:260,h:170,alt:"Graph of [R] concentration against time: a straight line falling from [R0] on the vertical axis, labelled k = \u2013slope.",d:[["l",90,150,90,12,"a"],["l",90,150,255,150,"a"],["t",98,22,"[R\u2080]",""],["l",90,30,215,110,""],["t",165,66,"k = \u2013slope",""],["t",84,76,"[R]","e"],["t",84,90,"concentration","es"],["l",140,162,170,162,"a"],["t",176,166,"time",""]]},sfig:{w:260,h:170,alt:"Graph of [R] concentration against time: a straight line falling from [R0] on the vertical axis, labelled k = \u2013slope.",d:[["l",90,150,90,12,"a"],["l",90,150,255,150,"a"],["t",98,22,"[R\u2080]",""],["l",90,30,215,110,""],["t",165,66,"k = \u2013slope",""],["t",84,76,"[R]","e"],["t",84,90,"concentration","es"],["l",140,162,170,162,"a"],["t",176,166,"time",""]]}},{id:"neet-2026-q088",pn:88,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Arrhenius equation",q:`Given below is an expression for the rate constant of a first-order reaction occurring at a certain temperature, T (K).
ln k = 14.34 \u2013 (1.25 \xD7 10\u2074)/T
The energy of activation in kcal mol\u207B\xB9 for the reaction is :
(Given: k in s\u207B\xB9, R = 1.987 cal mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9)`,o:["12.42","14.34","18.63","24.84"],c:3,s:`Arrhenius equation: k = A e^(\u2013Ea/RT).
ln k = ln A \u2013 Ea/(RT)
Comparing with the given expression, Ea/R = 1.25 \xD7 10\u2074.
Ea = 1.25 \xD7 10\u2074 \xD7 1.987 cal/mol
Ea = 24.84 kcal mol\u207B\xB9`},{id:"neet-2025-q056",pn:56,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"First order half-life",q:"If the half-life (t\u2081/\u2082) for a first order reaction is 1 minute, then the time required for 99.9% completion of the reaction is closest to :",o:["2 minutes","4 minutes","5 minutes","10 minutes"],c:3,s:`For a 1st order reaction: kt = 2.303 log (A\u2080/A\u209C), where A\u2080 is the initial concentration and A\u209C is the final concentration.
t\u2089\u2089.\u2089% = 10 t\u2081/\u2082.
t\u2089\u2089.\u2089% = 10 \xD7 1 minute = 10 minutes.`},{id:"neet-2025-q089",pn:89,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"First order rate constant and time",q:`If the rate constant of a reaction is 0.03 s\u207B\xB9, how much time does it take for 7.2 mol L\u207B\xB9 concentration of the reactant to get reduced to 0.9 mol L\u207B\xB9?
(Given: log 2 = 0.301)`,o:["69.3 s","23.1 s","210 s","21.0 s"],c:0,s:`k = 0.03 s\u207B\xB9
t = (2.303)/(k) log (a)/(a \u2212 x)
t = (2.303)/(0.03) log (7.2)/(0.9)
t = (2.303)/(0.03) log 8
t = (2.303)/(0.03) \xD7 3 \xD7 log 2
t = (2.303)/(0.03) \xD7 3 \xD7 0.301
t = 69.3 s`}]})();
